# Game Server Rules

## Project Goal

This project focuses on building a custom authoritative FPS server.

The server is the core of the project.

Primary goals:
- Build a high-performance IOCP-based game server
- Handle authoritative gameplay validation
- Maintain stable real-time synchronization
- Support scalable multiplayer simulation
- Minimize engine dependency

The server must remain fully independent from Unreal Engine.



# Phase 1 Goal (Primary Focus)

Phase 1 is focused on proving that the server can properly manage a real-time FPS environment.

The initial target is not visual quality.

The target is:
- synchronization
- validation
- stability
- networking
- architecture
- scalability



# Required Features

## Core Networking
- IOCP networking
- Session management
- Packet dispatcher
- Packet serialization/deserialization
- Protobuf integration
- Connection lifecycle handling

## Gameplay
- Movement validation
- Shooting validation
- Hit validation
- Health processing
- Basic collision handling
- Room-based simulation

## Synchronization
- Tick-based simulation
- Snapshot generation
- Snapshot broadcasting
- Interpolation support
- Basic prediction correction

## Concurrency
- Multithreaded worker structure
- JobQueue-based processing
- Thread-safe room handling
- Minimal lock usage

## Testing
- CLI server execution
- Fake client simulation
- Bot-based stress testing
- Packet logging



# Server Authority Rules

The server is always the source of truth.

Clients are never trusted for gameplay state.

## Client Can
- send input
- request actions
- predict temporary movement

## Client Cannot
- determine final position
- validate hits
- apply damage
- determine weapon state
- resolve gameplay results

All gameplay decisions must be validated server-side.



# Architecture Rules

## Keep Systems Independent

Game Server must remain runnable without:
- Unreal Engine
- rendering
- UI
- external tools

The server should be fully testable through CLI execution alone.



# Room Ownership Rules

Each room should behave like an isolated simulation unit.

A room should manage:
- players
- bots
- projectiles
- collision state
- tick updates
- job execution

Avoid unnecessary cross-room dependencies.



# Tick Rules

Gameplay must run on a fixed server tick.

Examples:
- movement update
- collision validation
- projectile update
- snapshot generation

Do NOT depend on variable frame timing for gameplay logic.



# Networking Rules

## Prefer Explicit Systems

Use:
- explicit packet structures
- explicit serialization
- deterministic flow
- predictable ownership

Avoid:
- hidden state mutation
- implicit synchronization
- unclear packet responsibility



# Validation Rules

All important gameplay actions require validation.

Required validation examples:
- movement speed
- teleport detection
- fire rate
- hit range
- invalid packet values

Never trust raw client data.



# Performance Rules

Always consider:
- packet throughput
- tick cost
- allocation frequency
- lock contention
- memory fragmentation
- thread ownership

Performance is part of the core design.

Do not optimize only after completion.



# Multithreading Rules

Prefer:
- ownership-based execution
- room isolation
- job queues
- deterministic processing

Avoid:
- excessive mutex usage
- global shared state
- unclear thread ownership

Thread ownership must always be understandable.



# Code Style Rules

## Naming

Use clear and understandable names.

Bad:
```cpp
DoThing()
Proc()
Tmp()
Calc()
```

Good:
```cpp
HandleMovementPacket()
ValidateHitResult()
BroadcastRoomSnapshot()
```

Avoid abbreviations unless universally understood.

Allowed:
- HP
- ID
- FPS
- UDP
- TCP
- IOCP

Avoid unclear shortened names.



# Function Rules

## Function Length

Target:
- Under 30 lines preferred
- Under 50 lines maximum

Split large logic into smaller responsibilities.

## Single Responsibility

One function should perform one clear task.

Bad:
```cpp
UpdatePlayerAndValidateAndBroadcast()
```

Good:
```cpp
UpdatePlayerMovement()
ValidateMovementState()
BroadcastSnapshot()
```

# Class Rules

## Keep Classes Focused

One class should have one responsibility.

Avoid:
- massive manager classes
- mixed networking/gameplay/database logic
- god objects

Prefer:
- isolated systems
- explicit ownership
- predictable flow


# Memory Rules

Avoid:
- unnecessary allocations
- uncontrolled dynamic memory usage
- frequent object recreation

Prefer:
- pooling
- reusable buffers
- stable ownership
- predictable lifetime management

# Debugging Rules

Every important system must be observable.

Required:
- packet logs
- room state logs
- tick timing logs
- disconnect logs
- validation failure logs

Synchronization problems must be traceable.

# Scope Control

Do NOT prioritize:
- web dashboard polish
- advanced database systems
- unnecessary tools
- overengineered abstractions

Prioritize:
- networking
- validation
- synchronization
- scalability
- stability
- debugging
- performance

